TASK 3 – Define the Constants (3 Marks)
1. Brief explanation
Constants store values that should remain unchanged during program execution. In the electricity billing system, tariff rates, slab limits and fixed charges should be defined in one place so that a tariff revision updates every bill consistently.
Types of constants in C
Feature	#define	const variable	enum
Purpose	Defines macros	Defines typed, read-only objects	Defines named integer constants
Type checking	No inherent type	Yes	Integer constants
Scope	Preprocessor substitution; not governed by C variable scope	Follows normal C scope rules	Enumerator names follow C scope rules
Debugging	Often not shown as a variable	Can be visible in a debugger	Names may be available to debugging tools
Storage	No storage for the macro itself	May occupy storage, depending on use	Enumerator values do not require separate storage
Best use	Tariff values and slab limits	Typed configuration values	Categories and status codes

Naming convention: Use uppercase letters and underscores, such as RATE2_PAISE, SLAB1_UNITS and FIXED_CHARGE_PAISE.

For this program, #define is suitable because the task specifically requires macros and allows a tariff revision in one place. For a larger application, const can provide stronger type checking.

CODE:


#include <stdio.h>

#define SLAB1_UNITS 100
#define SLAB2_UNITS 200
#define RATE1_PAISE 0
#define RATE2_PAISE 225
#define RATE3_PAISE 450
#define FIXED_CHARGE_PAISE 5000L

/* Returns the bill in paise */
long compute_bill(int units)
{
    long paise = FIXED_CHARGE_PAISE;

    // Charge for the first slab
    if (units > 0 && units <= SLAB1_UNITS)
        paise += (long)units * RATE1_PAISE;

    // Charge for units 101 to 200
    if (units > SLAB1_UNITS)
    {
        int slab2 = units < SLAB2_UNITS
                        ? units - SLAB1_UNITS
                        : SLAB2_UNITS - SLAB1_UNITS;

        paise += (long)slab2 * RATE2_PAISE;
    }

    // Charge for units above 200
    if (units > SLAB2_UNITS)
        paise += (long)(units - SLAB2_UNITS) * RATE3_PAISE;

    return paise;
}

int main(void)
{
    int tests[] = {0, 100, 150, 200, 350};
    int n = sizeof tests / sizeof tests[0];

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        long p = compute_bill(tests[i]);

        printf("%3d units -> Rs %ld.%02ld\n",
               tests[i], p / 100, p % 100);
    }

    return 0;
}

OUTPUT:

  0 units -> Rs 50.00
100 units -> Rs 50.00
150 units -> Rs 162.50
200 units -> Rs 275.00
350 units -> Rs 950.00




